From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733: add the eDP/DP bus gate and reset

The eDP/DP transmitter (eDP0 @0x05740000) is the block a DisplayPort-alt-mode
driver has to reach, and this tree cannot reach it: our CCU models no display
clock and no display reset at all, so EDP_BGR_REG keeps its reset value and the
whole eDP0 register window reads back zero. Measured on the 8 GiB board on our own
6.18.36: 0x0200364C = 0x00000000, against
0x00010001 under the vendor 6.6 stack on the same board while it drove a live
four-lane DP-alt link. This patch models exactly that one register,
so a future DP transmitter driver can claim it. It changes nothing by itself --
nothing in the tree references the new ids yet.

Where the values come from
--------------------------
  CCU base            0x02002000        A733 user manual, CCU register list
  EDP_BGR_REG         offset 0x164C     A733 UM V0.92 4.1.6.228 (offset 5708),
  absolute            0x0200364C        CCU base + offset
  bit 0  EDP_GATING   0=Mask 1=Pass     EDP_BGR_REG field table
  bit 16 EDP_RST      0=Assert          (same table)
                      1=De-assert
  reset value         0x00000000        same table, and read back as such on the 8 GiB board

Silicon, three reads, none of them from code:
  - the 6 GiB board, vendor stack, 2026-07-18: 0x0200364c = 0x00010001, the only released
    display gate on that board
  - the 8 GiB board, vendor 6.6, 2026-08-17, live 4-lane DP link: the same 0x00010001
  - the 8 GiB board, our 6.18.36, 2026-08-18: 0x00000000

Both bits, not one -- measured
------------------------------
The gate alone does nothing observable. Written by hand on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-18
and restored afterwards:

  0x0200364C = 0x00000001   gate only            -> 0x057400FC reads 0x00000000
  0x0200364C = 0x00010001   gate + reset released -> 0x057400FC reads 0x000A0509

0x000A0509 is the DP core id the UM documents, and the same value the vendor
shows on a live link. That is why the clock and the reset are one patch and why
the pending observable below is the core id rather than a CCU read-back: the CCU
echoing what was just written to it proves only that the write landed.

And the block does more than answer once it is up. On the same board and day,
with these two bits released by hand and the AUX front end powered by `phy-allwinner-a733-aux-hpd-phy` and `phy-allwinner-a733-aux-hpd-bringup-knob`,
a hand-driven AUX transaction read DPCD 0x00000 = 12 0A 84 ... (DPCD rev 1.2,
MAX_LINK_RATE 2.7 Gbps, 4 lanes) and a full checksum-valid EDID over
I2C-over-AUX. That is the
whole point of modelling this register -- the sink is reachable the moment the
gate and the reset are.

What the A523 says, and why none of it was taken
------------------------------------------------
ccu-sun55i-a523.c is the nearest mainline part and it models this block, which is
exactly what makes it dangerous here -- every number in it is plausible and every
number in it is wrong for the A733:

  A523 (ccu-sun55i-a523.c:1032,1618)   A733 (UM 4.1.6.228)
  bus_edp     0xbbc BIT(0)             EDP_BGR_REG 0x164C bit0
  RST_BUS_EDP 0xbbc BIT(16)            EDP_BGR_REG 0x164C bit16

Neither 0x0BB0 nor 0x0BBC is a documented A733 CCU register: both are absent from
the register list of the A733 user manual, whose nearest neighbours are
0x0B24 (GPU0_GATING_REG) and 0x0C00 (DRAM0_CLK_REG). Carried over unchanged, the
gate would write into an undocumented hole and the eDP block would stay dark
while the write looked like it had succeeded, which is this tree's most expensive
failure mode.

What is deliberately left out: the EDP_TV module clock
------------------------------------------------------
EDP_TV_CLK_REG (0x1640) is NOT modelled, and the omission is a
decision, not an oversight:

  - It is not needed for register access. During the measurement above it stayed
    0x00000000 the whole time while the eDP0 window answered. Its job is pixel
    transport, not reachability -- so it belongs to the patch that drives pixels,
    not to this one.
  - Its mux cannot be expressed by this tree yet. CLK_SRC_SEL [26:24] selects
    000 VIDEO0PLL4X, 001 VIDEO1PLL4X, 010 VIDEO2PLL4X, 011 PERI0PLL2X, and this
    CCU models none of the three video PLLs. The one parent silicon shows in use
    is VIDEO1PLL4X (EDP_TV_CLK = 0x81000000 on the 6 GiB board)
    -- precisely the one we cannot name. Modelling a four-way mux with three
    parents missing would be a guess, and guesses do not ship here.

For the patch that does model it later, the field widths are read and recorded so
nobody re-derives them: bit 31 gating, [26:24] the four-way mux above, [12:8]
FACTOR_N with N = FACTOR_N + 1, [4:0] FACTOR_M with M = FACTOR_M + 1, both 5 bits
wide, EDP_TV_CLK = source / M / N. The A523's edp clock at
0xbb0 is a 4-bit M with no N and five parents -- it must not be copied either.

Also left out, and named so they are not mistaken for gaps in this patch:
TCONTV1_BGR_REG (0x160C) and DPSS_TOP1_BGR_REG (0x16CC) are separate gates with
separate resets, and they belong to the TCON_TV and DPSS patches.

The new ids do not collide: allocated against main, next free clock id
59 and next free reset id 24, and scripts/check-binding-ids.sh is green over all
eight A733 headers (60 clock ids, 25 reset ids, no duplicates in any namespace).

Verified: applies at fuzz 0 as part of the full series, ccu-sun60iw2.o builds
warning-free at W=1, and checkpatch --strict reports 0 checks and the same three
findings the other CCU patches in this series produce -- the 75-char description
note, this tree's fixed Signed-off-by line, and "DT includes should be a separate
patch" -- and no new ones.

NOT hardware-proven. What would prove it, stated as one observable: with a
consumer node claiming clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_EDP> and resets = <&ccu
RST_BUS_EDP> and both taken, 0x057400FC reads 0x000A0509 on the 8 GiB board AND a native
AUX read of DPCD 0 returns the sink's capability bytes -- both of which the
hand-driven session above has already produced, so what is unproven is only that
our driver reaches the same state, not that the state exists. 0x0200364C reading
0x00010001 is the necessary intermediate step, not the proof.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-ccu.h
+++ b/include/dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-ccu.h
@@ -81,4 +81,7 @@
 #define CLK_BUS_PWM0		57
 #define CLK_BUS_PWM1		58
 
+/* eDP/DP transmitter bus gate -- EDP_BGR_REG 0x164C bit0. */
+#define CLK_BUS_EDP		59
+
 #endif /* _DT_BINDINGS_CLK_SUN60I_A733_CCU_H_ */
--- a/include/dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-ccu.h
+++ b/include/dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-ccu.h
@@ -37,4 +37,7 @@
 #define RST_BUS_PWM0		22
 #define RST_BUS_PWM1		23
 
+/* eDP/DP transmitter reset -- EDP_BGR_REG 0x164C bit16. */
+#define RST_BUS_EDP		24
+
 #endif /* _DT_BINDINGS_RST_SUN60I_A733_CCU_H_ */
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60iw2.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60iw2.c
@@ -473,6 +473,37 @@
 static SUNXI_CCU_GATE_HWS(bus_pwm1_clk,   "bus-pwm1",   ahb_hws, 0x078C, BIT(0),  0);
 static SUNXI_CCU_GATE_HWS(mbus_gmac0_clk, "mbus-gmac0", ahb_hws, 0x05E4, BIT(11), 0);
 
+/*
+ * eDP/DP transmitter (eDP0 @0x05740000, the DP-alt sink of the COMB0 combo PHY).
+ *
+ *   EDP_BGR_REG  0x164C  bit0 EDP_GATING (0=Mask 1=Pass)
+ *                        bit16 EDP_RST   (0=Assert 1=De-assert), reset 0
+ *
+ * A733 UM V0.92 4.1.6.228: offset 0x164C (5708) in the CCU register list,
+ * field layout from that register's table in the A733 user manual; absolute
+ * address 0x0200364C over the CCU base 0x02002000.
+ *
+ * Both bits are needed and that is measured, not reasoned: on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-18
+ * writing 0x00000001 (gate only) left the entire 0x05740000 window reading zero,
+ * and 0x00010001 (gate + reset de-asserted) made 0x057400FC return 0x000A0509,
+ * the core id the UM documents.
+ * So a consumer has to take the clock AND the reset; either alone proves nothing.
+ * With both released, a hand-driven AUX read on the same board returned the
+ * sink's DPCD and a checksum-valid EDID
+ *.
+ *
+ * The module clock EDP_TV_CLK_REG (0x1640) is deliberately NOT modelled here;
+ * the patch header says why, and the same measurement shows it is not needed for
+ * register access (it stayed 0x00000000 throughout).
+ *
+ * The bus parent is an inference and is marked as one: the UM names the register
+ * but not which bus feeds the eDP block. ahb follows what this tree already does
+ * for every peripheral BGR gate (bus-smhc0, bus-serdes, bus-gmac0, bus-ths). It
+ * is inert for correctness -- nothing derives a rate from a BGR gate; the eDP
+ * pixel rate comes from EDP_TV_CLK, which is not modelled.
+ */
+static SUNXI_CCU_GATE_HWS(bus_edp_clk,    "bus-edp",    ahb_hws, 0x164C, BIT(0),  0);
+
 /* ccu_common-based clocks (registered and reset-mapped by the core). */
 static struct ccu_common *sun60iw2_ccu_clks[] = {
 	&pll_ref_clk.common,
@@ -520,6 +551,7 @@
 	&gpadc0_24m_clk.common,
 	&bus_pwm0_clk.common,
 	&bus_pwm1_clk.common,
+	&bus_edp_clk.common,
 	&mbus_gmac0_clk.common,
 };
 
@@ -584,8 +616,9 @@
 		[CLK_GPADC0_24M]	= &gpadc0_24m_clk.common.hw,
 		[CLK_BUS_PWM0]		= &bus_pwm0_clk.common.hw,
 		[CLK_BUS_PWM1]		= &bus_pwm1_clk.common.hw,
+		[CLK_BUS_EDP]		= &bus_edp_clk.common.hw,
 	},
-	.num	= CLK_BUS_PWM1 + 1,
+	.num	= CLK_BUS_EDP + 1,
 };
 
 static struct ccu_reset_map sun60iw2_ccu_resets[] = {
@@ -613,6 +646,7 @@
 	[RST_BUS_THS]		= { 0x0FE4, BIT(16) },
 	[RST_BUS_PWM0]		= { 0x0784, BIT(16) },
 	[RST_BUS_PWM1]		= { 0x078C, BIT(16) },
+	[RST_BUS_EDP]		= { 0x164C, BIT(16) },
 };
 
 static const struct sunxi_ccu_desc sun60iw2_ccu_desc = {
